definition:
A conclusion is the final integration of the integrated message developed throughout the paragraph.

The conclusion is not merely the final sentence.

Nor is it merely a restatement of the thesis.

Rather, the conclusion completes the communicative act initiated by the thesis and progressively developed through elaboration.

The conclusion expresses the understanding that the reader should possess after the developmental work of the paragraph has been completed.

Different communicative purposes require different forms of final integration.

For example, a conclusion may:

• demonstrate how the elaboration has fulfilled the communicative promise established by the thesis;

• integrate the major ideas developed throughout the paragraph into a more coherent understanding;

• extend the thesis by identifying broader implications, applications, or consequences;

• answer the question posed by the thesis more fully than the thesis alone could;

• resolve a tension, comparison, or problem developed during elaboration;

• identify the larger significance of the integrated message when appropriate;

• bring the intellectual activity to a natural point of completion.

These are not fixed conclusion types.

They are different ways of achieving the same developmental goal:

Helping the reader leave the paragraph with a more integrated understanding than when they entered it.

function:
The conclusion performs the final intellectual work of the paragraph.

Its functions include:

• integrating the understanding developed throughout the paragraph;

• completing the communicative purpose established by the thesis;

• expressing the understanding that has emerged through elaboration;

• bringing the paragraph to an intellectually satisfying point of completion.

The conclusion should communicate the understanding that has emerged through the paragraph rather than merely repeat where the paragraph began.

discovery_instruction:
Compass teaches:

• what conclusions are;

• why conclusions complete rather than repeat;

• how conclusions express the understanding produced through elaboration;

• the different ways conclusions may achieve final integration;

• how conclusions bring intellectual closure.

Compass compares the learner's work with these structural requirements.

Compass never teaches that conclusions simply restate the thesis.
